#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151914 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152104 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e3e8809-2164-543d-bb0a-c6a20cc7de6b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Rate as the Quotient of an Integer and a Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "67b3b574-0346-5133-9f0b-303bc00ace78" :question/math-academy-id "e-4450" :question/problem "Jamie's family drove $15$ miles on $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ of a gallon of gas. What is the unit rate of miles per gallon of gas?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the unit rate of miles per gallon of gas, we divide the number of miles, $15,$ by the number of gallons of gas, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n15 \\div \\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\nTo divide by a fraction, we multiply by the fraction's reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15÷\\frac{3}{4} & =15\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{1}\\times \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{15\\times 4}{1\\times 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{60}{3} \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the unit rate is $20$ miles per gallon of gas."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151915 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152105 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b04f86ac-4028-5df6-8913-44ef091ed396" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Rate as the Quotient of a Fraction and an Integer"}] :question/id #uuid "1d6f2a78-51e4-55cf-9c75-79a4624d630c" :question/math-academy-id "e-4451" :question/problem "Bill measured $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ tablespoon of olive oil and found that it weighed $4$ grams. How many tablespoons is $1$ gram of olive oil?" :question/worked-solution "To find the number of tablespoons in $1$ gram of olive oil, we can compute the unit rate of tablespoons per gram olive oil. We do this by dividing the number of tablespoons, $\\dfrac{1}{3},$ by the number of grams, $4,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\div 4\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by a whole number is equivalent to multiplying the fraction by the reciprocal of that whole number:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}÷4 & =\\frac{1}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\times 1}{3\\times 4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{12}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, there is $\\dfrac{1}{12}$ of a tablespoon in $1$ gram of olive oil."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151916 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152106 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "87385cd2-8143-54de-9995-e1cc2ecf06c0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Rate as the Quotient of a Fraction and a Fraction"}] :question/id #uuid "f830d531-b8f0-5b62-88a5-38edf6459b55" :question/math-academy-id "e-4452" :question/problem "Martha swam $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ of a mile in $\\dfrac{2}{3}$ of an hour. How many miles can Martha swim in $1$ hour?" :question/worked-solution "To find the number of miles that Martha can swim in $1$ hour, we can compute the unit rate of miles per hour. We do this by dividing the number of miles, $\\dfrac{3}{4},$ by the number of hours, $\\dfrac{2}{3},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\div \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\nDividing a fraction by another fraction is equivalent to multiplying the first fraction by the reciprocal of the second fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{4}÷\\frac{2}{3} & =\\frac{3}{4}\\times \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\times 3}{4\\times 2} \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write the resulting improper fraction as a mixed number:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{8} = 1 \\, \\textrm{R} 1 = 1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Martha can swim $1 \\, \\dfrac{1}{8}$ miles in $1$ hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186557227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152104 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2e3e8809-2164-543d-bb0a-c6a20cc7de6b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Unit Rate as the Quotient of an Integer and a Fraction"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d089f51-d83a-525c-987a-61fa02546ef1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$\\frac{96}{5}$ exercises" :db/id #